Q: How to catch an assert with Google test? A: <p>EXPECT_FATAL_FAILURE(statement,text) and EXPECT_NONFATAL_FAILURE(statement,text) will <strong>only pass</strong> if 'statement' invokes a <strong>failing</strong> ASSERT_x or EXECT_x respectively.</p>
<p>These statements will pass in your tests:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>EXPECT_NONFATAL_FAILURE( EXPECT_TRUE( 0 ), &quot;&quot; );
EXPECT_FATAL_FAILURE( ASSERT_TRUE( 0 ), &quot;&quot; );
</code></pre>

Q: C++ ranges::elements_of(ranges::iota_view(0, 11)); }() | ranges::reverse_view() | ranges::to&lt;vector&gt;() A: <p>Even fixing the syntax for using <code>reverse_view</code>, either <code>generator | std::views::reverse</code> or <code>std::ranges::reverse_view(generator)</code>, you will find compile errors.</p>
<p>You can't reverse a <code>std::generator</code>, because it doesn't model <code>std::ranges::bidirectional_range</code>. <code>std::generator</code> is a single-pass, forward-only range.</p>
<p>You haven't shown anything requiring <code>std::generator</code> here.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::vector&lt;std::size_t&gt; decreasing(std::size_t size) {
    return std::views::iota(0z, size) | std::views::reverse | std::ranges::to&lt;std::vector&gt;();
}

INSTANTIATE_TEST_SUITE_P(
    MyTests,
    MyTestFixture,
    ::testing::Values(make_tuple(1, decreasing(1)),
                      make_tuple(1, decreasing(11))));
</code></pre>
